WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
So imagine you're at this really crowded party.

2
00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.520
You know, the music is a little too loud,

3
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.360
people are laughing, there's glasses clinking everywhere.

4
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:08.800
And you look across the room

5
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.080
and you notice three different people.

6
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.760
And all of them are standing completely alone,

7
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.460
just keeping their distance from the crowd.

8
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.660
On the surface, if you were just looking

9
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.520
at a snapshot of this, you would say

10
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.120
they are doing the exact same thing.

11
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.320
Yeah, the observable behavior is, well, it's identical.

12
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.320
I mean, they are physically isolating themselves

13
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.480
and keeping people at arm's length.

14
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.840
But the foundational principle of psychology

15
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.560
tells us that identical behaviors frequently mask

16
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.560
profoundly different internal motivations.

17
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.800
So two people can be standing alone at a party,

18
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.000
but, you know, one is terrified

19
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.320
and the other is just perfectly at peace.

20
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.960
Which brings us to the mission for today's deep dive.

21
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:47.800
Yeah.

22
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.320
We've got a stack of clinical diagnostic manuals here,

23
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.480
specifically the ICD 10 and 11

24
00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.000
alongside some treatment literature

25
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.400
and psychological case studies.

26
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.080
Some really dense reading, yeah.

27
00:00:59.080 --> 00:00:59.920
Exactly.

28
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.040
And we are unpacking a specific group

29
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.160
of psychological profiles known as

30
00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.600
the cluster A personality disorders.

31
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.240
Right, which are clinically grouped

32
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.240
as the odd and eccentric personalities.

33
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.200
Yes, odd and eccentric.

34
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.780
And our goal for you, the listener,

35
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:18.800
is to understand the core mystery of that party scenario.

36
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.080
We wanna look past the physical distance

37
00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.720
and figure out the mechanical why behind the isolation.

38
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.800
Because understanding that why

39
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.040
is the ultimate separator.

40
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.760
I mean, if you just catalog the behavior,

41
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.840
you completely miss the structural architecture

42
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.720
of the mind that's actually producing it.

43
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:34.720
Okay, let's unpack this.

44
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.960
We'll start with the first person standing alone

45
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.560
at that party.

46
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:40.520
This is paranoid personality disorder.

47
00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.000
Officially coded in the diagnostic manual as F60.0.

48
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.520
Right, F60.0.

49
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.080
And the distance here is manufactured by fear and mistrust.

50
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.360
In reading through the case studies,

51
00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.680
the best way I can conceptualize this is,

52
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.080
it's like walking through your entire life

53
00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.840
wearing a massive, heavy, medieval suit of armor.

54
00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.160
That's a really good way to put it.

55
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.560
You wear it to the grocery store,

56
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.480
you wear it to work, you wear it to sleep.

57
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.680
And yeah, it keeps you safe from incoming arrows,

58
00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.680
but it makes it physically impossible to hug anyone.

59
00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.440
But you know, that armor isn't empty.

60
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.240
Inside that heavy suit is a hyper-vigilant nervous system

61
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.360
that just never, ever powers down.

62
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.200
Never.

63
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.720
The diagnostic criteria actually specify

64
00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.920
a pervasive, distressed, and suspiciousness of others.

65
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.000
And pervasive is the key word there.

66
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.360
Meaning it colors literally every interaction.

67
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.200
Exactly.

68
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.480
This is a baseline neurological assumption

69
00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.680
that the world and everyone in it

70
00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.120
is actively operating with hidden motives

71
00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.120
to exploit, harm, or deceive them.

72
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.640
And the literature really emphasizes

73
00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.400
how they read hidden threats

74
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.560
into just the most ordinary remarks.

75
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.160
Like if I walk up to this person at the party

76
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.400
and say, hey, nice shirt.

77
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.080
They aren't processing that as a compliment.

78
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:49.040
No, not at all.

79
00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.520
Their internal radar just immediately scans

80
00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.040
that benign comment for the threat.

81
00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.840
They're thinking, why is he focusing on my clothes?

82
00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:56.880
Is he mocking my weight?

83
00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.320
Is it some kind of set up for a joke at my expense?

84
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:00.160
Wow.

85
00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.520
And because that threat matrix is constantly running,

86
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.360
they hold these incredibly deep grudges

87
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.440
and they refuse to confine in anyone

88
00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.760
because information is simply ammunition.

89
00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.560
Right, any personal preference, any vulnerability,

90
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.960
any secret you share is immediately cataloged

91
00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.320
by their brain as a weapon handed to the enemy.

92
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.640
Which sounds exhausting.

93
00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.480
It is.

94
00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.240
It's an incredibly rigid psychological posture.

95
00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:25.880
Which brings up a glaring paradox

96
00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.440
in the clinical treatment literature that I saw.

97
00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.520
The manuals list psychotherapy as the first line treatment.

98
00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:32.480
They do.

99
00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.560
But engaging the patient seems mechanically impossible.

100
00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.640
I mean, therapy requires sitting in a room,

101
00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.760
making yourself vulnerable to a stranger,

102
00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.440
and building a therapeutic alliance.

103
00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.320
You're asking someone to take off their armor

104
00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:45.520
in front of a clinician

105
00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:46.920
when their brain is screaming

106
00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.640
that the clinician is holding a sword.

107
00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.240
Yeah, that is the ultimate clinical irony.

108
00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.360
So how does a professional even bypass that defense system?

109
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.080
Well, you don't try to disarm them.

110
00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.000
I mean, trying to force emotional vulnerability

111
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.280
or demanding trust from a patient with F60.0

112
00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.440
will just instantly rupture the relationship.

113
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:04.320
So what do you do?

114
00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.160
Instead, clinicians utilize this mechanism

115
00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.200
of radical, almost clinical transparency.

116
00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.400
They focus entirely on practical, somatic,

117
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.760
or external life stressors.

118
00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.240
So if the patient is stressed about a landlord,

119
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.800
the therapist focuses on the landlord.

120
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:22.080
Okay, keeping it external.

121
00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:22.920
Right.

122
00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.000
Furthermore, the therapist externalizes their own process.

123
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.960
They might say, I'm gonna take notes now.

124
00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.760
I am writing down what you said about your job

125
00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:30.720
so I don't forget.

126
00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:31.600
Oh, wow.

127
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.120
So you literally narrate what you're doing.

128
00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.680
Exactly, you eliminate shadows.

129
00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:37.320
Over a very long timeline,

130
00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.880
the therapist kind of becomes a neutral fixture

131
00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.520
in the patient's environment rather than a threat.

132
00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.160
It's essentially treating the relationship

133
00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.340
like a highly formalized contract

134
00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:47.880
rather than an emotional bond.

135
00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.080
That's exactly it.

136
00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.520
But navigating that requires strict clinical boundaries,

137
00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.760
especially regarding the diagnostic traps

138
00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.600
mentioned in our sources.

139
00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.440
The literature draws a very hard line

140
00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.000
between paranoid personality disorder

141
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.280
and actual delusional disorders,

142
00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.520
or, you know, poderomal schizophrenia.

143
00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.360
We aren't talking about fixed, bizarre delusions here.

144
00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.280
No, the boundary is reality testing.

145
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.360
Paranoid personality is an ingrained lifelong pattern

146
00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:16.480
of viewing the world

147
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:20.040
through a deeply cynical, mistrustful lens.

148
00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.720
But the patient is not experiencing frank psychosis.

149
00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:25.200
They aren't hallucinating voices

150
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.160
commanding them to isolate.

151
00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.720
If they interpret your comment

152
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:30.240
about their shirt as an insult,

153
00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.840
that is a distortion of intent.

154
00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:35.460
But if they genuinely believe the shirt is woven

155
00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.960
with microchips transmitting their thoughts to the CIA,

156
00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.240
well, the suspicion has hardened into a fixed delusion.

157
00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.560
At that point, you have officially crossed the border

158
00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.960
out of personality disorders entirely.

159
00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:48.720
So the person in the armor at the party

160
00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.020
knows it's a party.

161
00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:53.080
They know you are just a person holding a cup of punch.

162
00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:54.760
They just assume you're gonna throw the punch

163
00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:56.320
in their face the second they look away.

164
00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:57.160
Precisely.

165
00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.360
It is a severe misinterpretation of social data,

166
00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.680
not a hallucination of data that just isn't there.

167
00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:05.760
Okay, so if the first person is trapped

168
00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.480
inside a fortress of their own making, out of fear,

169
00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.480
let's look at a mind that doesn't even want the fortress,

170
00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:11.760
the kingdom, or the people in it.

171
00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.120
The second person at the party.

172
00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.520
Right, the second person standing along.

173
00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:18.800
This represents schizoid personality disorder,

174
00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.320
code F60BOLT11.

175
00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:22.960
And the distance here is driven

176
00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:25.520
by sheer unadulterated indifference.

177
00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.000
Indifference is the perfect word.

178
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.040
It really is.

179
00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.400
This profile pushes hard against the assumption

180
00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.040
that human beings are universally driven

181
00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:35.400
by a need for connection.

182
00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.440
It's like someone who genuinely lacks

183
00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.320
the taste receptors for chocolate,

184
00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.440
living in a world that assumes absolutely

185
00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:42.720
everyone has a sweet tooth.

186
00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.840
Right, and society relentlessly offers them chocolate

187
00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.960
and then pathologizes their refusal.

188
00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:48.800
Exactly.

189
00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.400
The defining mechanism of schizoid personality disorder

190
00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.480
is profound social detachment paired

191
00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:55.800
with a restricted emotional effect.

192
00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.320
I mean, they are indifferent to praise,

193
00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.320
immune to criticism, and experience zero distress

194
00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.680
regarding their isolation.

195
00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:03.720
That's wild to me.

196
00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.120
Well, the social anhedonia is egosyntonic,

197
00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.040
meaning it aligns entirely with their own self-image

198
00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:11.400
and desires, they don't feel broken.

199
00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:12.680
See, I have to challenge the idea

200
00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:13.800
of complete indifference here,

201
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.840
just looking at this from an evolutionary biology perspective.

202
00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:19.640
Humans are biologically wired as pack animals, right?

203
00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:21.800
Our nervous systems regulate through connection

204
00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.200
because, historically, isolation

205
00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.720
literally meant death by predation.

206
00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.840
So is it truly possible that a schizoid individual

207
00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:33.240
has absolutely zero biological appetite for connection?

208
00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:35.840
Or is the literature suggesting it's just suppressed

209
00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:39.140
beneath some impenetrable dissociative layer?

210
00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:41.320
It's a great question, but the clinical consensus

211
00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:45.040
leans toward a structural neurodevelopmental deficit

212
00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.160
in the actual social reward circuitry.

213
00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:48.200
Really? Yeah.

214
00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.520
When you or I have a positive social interaction,

215
00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:54.120
our brains release this cascade of oxytocin and dopamine.

216
00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:54.960
We feel warmth.

217
00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.120
The schizoid brain just does not produce

218
00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.000
or receive that same chemical reward.

219
00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:00.520
Oh, wow, so the hardware is different.

220
00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:01.480
Exactly.

221
00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:03.200
The hardware processing social connection

222
00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:05.460
simply doesn't fire the same way.

223
00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.480
It isn't that they are actively suppressing

224
00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:09.140
a desperate need for a hug,

225
00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:11.500
it's that the hug registers as entirely neutral

226
00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:13.140
or even just physically intrusive

227
00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:15.460
without any emotional resonance.

228
00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:16.840
Here's where it gets really interesting

229
00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:18.740
in the diagnostic literature.

230
00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:21.580
The sources highlight a major trap here,

231
00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:24.580
which is confusing schizoid personality disorder

232
00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:26.740
with avoidant personality disorder,

233
00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:28.580
which sits over in cluster C.

234
00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.100
Right, very common mix-up.

235
00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:32.300
Because if you only look at the behavior,

236
00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:35.260
both individuals are sitting alone in their apartments

237
00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:38.380
on a Friday night, seemingly doing the exact same thing.

238
00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:39.940
Right, but the distinction comes down

239
00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:43.740
to the presence or absence of an internal drive.

240
00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:44.940
Desire, essentially.

241
00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.100
Yes, the schizoid person genuinely lacks

242
00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:49.640
the appetite for connection.

243
00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:50.820
They are reading a book,

244
00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.100
perfectly at baseline, completely content.

245
00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:55.700
The avoidant person, on the other hand,

246
00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:57.640
is starving for connection.

247
00:08:57.640 --> 00:08:58.740
But they're still alone.

248
00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:00.300
Yes, they're sitting on their couch,

249
00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:03.060
intensely tracking the party they weren't invited to,

250
00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:05.860
paralyzed by a hypersensitivity to rejection

251
00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:09.500
and this deep-seated belief in their own social inadequacy.

252
00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.740
So never confuse, prefers to be alone

253
00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.340
with afraid to be with people.

254
00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:14.340
Exactly.

255
00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:15.740
One is a lack of appetite,

256
00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:18.260
the other is starvation caused by fear.

257
00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:21.880
Knowing that, how does a clinician approach treatment

258
00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:23.500
for F60.1?

259
00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:24.340
Yeah.

260
00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:26.320
I mean, if the patient has no desire

261
00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.140
to change their social isolation

262
00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:30.060
and they don't feel the stress about it,

263
00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:34.060
what is the mechanical goal of supportive psychotherapy?

264
00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:35.220
You can't really spark a flame

265
00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:36.740
if there's no combustible material.

266
00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:37.580
You can't.

267
00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:39.180
So the goal shifts entirely away

268
00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:41.180
from generating emotional intimacy

269
00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:43.540
and moves toward functional adaptation.

270
00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:45.100
Just getting by in the world.

271
00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:45.940
Pretty much.

272
00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:49.340
The modern world demands social interaction for survival.

273
00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:51.180
You have to interview for jobs,

274
00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:52.740
navigate corporate hierarchies,

275
00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:54.140
interact with landlords.

276
00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:57.660
So the therapist acts almost like a sociological consultant.

277
00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.260
They teach the schizoid patient social mimicry.

278
00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:01.100
Social mimicry.

279
00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:03.480
Yeah, how to deploy appropriate eye contact,

280
00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:04.600
when to nod,

281
00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:07.680
how to utilize small talk as a tool to navigate the workplace

282
00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:09.600
without drawing negative attention.

283
00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:11.800
You weren't trying to make them crave the party,

284
00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:13.800
you were just teaching them how to walk through the room

285
00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.680
without bumping into the furniture.

286
00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:17.200
Okay, that makes a lot of sense.

287
00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.720
So we've covered distance caused

288
00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:20.880
by a hypervigilant fear of harm

289
00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:24.800
and distance caused by a profound neurological apathy.

290
00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:26.440
Now we look at the third person at the party.

291
00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:27.280
Right.

292
00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:29.160
This profile pairs physical distance

293
00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:32.120
with an entirely altered perception of reality.

294
00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.680
This is schizotypal personality disorder.

295
00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.160
And looking at the coding in the ICD manual,

296
00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:39.920
there is a glaring structural anomaly here.

297
00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:40.760
F21.

298
00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:41.880
Exactly.

299
00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:46.000
The first two we explored were F60.0 and F60.1.

300
00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:47.920
They sit squarely in the F60 block,

301
00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:50.540
which is dedicated to personality disorders.

302
00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:53.920
But schizotypal is yanked out of that neighborhood entirely

303
00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:55.760
and placed in the F21 block

304
00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:57.720
right alongside the schizophrenia spectrum.

305
00:10:57.720 --> 00:10:58.920
Yes, it is.

306
00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:00.480
Why the different categorization?

307
00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.000
I mean, what is mechanically happening

308
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:04.000
in this patient's mind that requires moving them

309
00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:05.760
into a different clinical zip code?

310
00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.520
Well, the placement reflects the presence

311
00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:09.680
of cognitive and perceptual distortions

312
00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:10.920
that you simply do not see

313
00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:12.520
in the other personality disorders.

314
00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:13.360
Like what?

315
00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:16.200
Schizotypal features the severe social detachment

316
00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.400
we saw in the schizoid profile,

317
00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:22.080
but it introduces this mechanism of cognitive slippage.

318
00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.520
Their brain's pattern recognition software

319
00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:27.400
is hyperactive and totally dysregulated.

320
00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:30.080
The sources refer to this as magical thinking

321
00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:31.880
and ideas of reference.

322
00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:34.080
Let's break down the mechanics of an idea of reference

323
00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:37.000
because it's a really fascinating neurological glitch.

324
00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:38.160
It really is.

325
00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:40.760
An idea of reference occurs when the brain assigns

326
00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:45.080
profound personalized salience to random neutral stimuli.

327
00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:45.920
Okay.

328
00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:49.280
Normally your brain filters out background noise, right?

329
00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:51.280
You drive past a billboard for a local bank,

330
00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.480
your brain categorizes it as irrelevant

331
00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:54.680
and you forget it instantly.

332
00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:55.800
Right, it's just noise.

333
00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:58.720
But a schizotypal brain fails to filter that stimulus.

334
00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:02.000
Instead, it flags the billboard as highly significant,

335
00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.600
leading the individual to believe

336
00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.720
the specific wording on the sign

337
00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:08.440
is a hidden message directed entirely at them.

338
00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:09.280
Wow.

339
00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.320
Yeah, perhaps instructing them

340
00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.440
on like a spiritual path for the day.

341
00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:15.080
So it's a deeply personalized mystical connection

342
00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:17.480
to just random environmental noise.

343
00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:20.000
The literature also notes they hold odd beliefs,

344
00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:21.840
have unusual processual experiences

345
00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:23.320
like feeling a strange energy

346
00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:25.720
or a presence in an empty room.

347
00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:28.520
And their speech patterns can be highly eccentric,

348
00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:31.160
relying on these loose associations or metaphors

349
00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:33.280
that only they really understand.

350
00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:35.720
And because their internal processing of reality

351
00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:38.400
is so fundamentally different from the baseline,

352
00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.680
interacting with the general public creates immense friction.

353
00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:43.240
Which explains the isolation.

354
00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:44.080
Right.

355
00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:45.520
The social distance they maintain

356
00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:48.160
is often a byproduct of this friction.

357
00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:50.880
They realize others do not see the world the way they do,

358
00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:52.560
which generates social anxiety

359
00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.280
that does not diminish with familiarity.

360
00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:56.880
This raises an important question

361
00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:00.320
for clinicians navigating the dual diagnostic traps here.

362
00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:03.560
Because schizotypal is walking a very thin tightrope.

363
00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:06.240
On one side, you have schizoid personality disorder

364
00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:08.920
because both feature severe social detachment.

365
00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:11.640
Right, but the eccentricity is the dividing line there.

366
00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:14.640
The schizoid person is detached and flat, right?

367
00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:16.440
They aren't experiencing magical thinking

368
00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:18.160
or perceptual oddities.

369
00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:19.520
They are just solitary.

370
00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:22.240
Schizotypal adds that distorted mystical layer.

371
00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:23.640
And on the other side of the tightrope,

372
00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.600
you have actual schizophrenia.

373
00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:27.400
Schizotypal is sitting right there

374
00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:30.520
in the F21 schizophrenia spectrum block.

375
00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:35.280
So mechanically, how is it not just a case of schizophrenia?

376
00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:38.400
It comes down to intensity, duration,

377
00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:40.880
and the preservation of reality testing.

378
00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:44.280
Schizotypal is clinically described as attenuated.

379
00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:45.120
Attenuated, okay.

380
00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.520
Yeah, meaning the symptoms are dialed down.

381
00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:49.800
The patient might feel a strange presence

382
00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:52.440
or believe they possess a sixth sense,

383
00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.440
but they are not experiencing sustained unmistakable

384
00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:56.680
hallucinations.

385
00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:59.200
So no auditory hallucinations telling them what to do.

386
00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:00.040
Exactly.

387
00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:01.680
They don't hear a distinct external voice

388
00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:03.000
commanding them to act,

389
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.200
and they do not suffer the catastrophic breakdown

390
00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:07.840
of cognitive function seen in acute schizophrenia.

391
00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:09.480
They touch the border of psychosis,

392
00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:11.400
but they don't cross over and live there.

393
00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:14.120
Which explains the shift in the treatment protocol.

394
00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:15.960
Psychotherapy for schizotypal focuses

395
00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:18.520
on reality testing and social skills training.

396
00:14:18.520 --> 00:14:20.520
But because we're dealing with cognitive slippage

397
00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:22.280
and dopamine dysregulation,

398
00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:24.640
the pharmacological approach changes.

399
00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:26.120
The sources indicate that treatment

400
00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:29.440
frequently includes low dose antipsychotic medications.

401
00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:30.400
It does, yeah.

402
00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:33.120
Mechanically, what is that medication

403
00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.600
doing to a schizotypal brain?

404
00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:38.320
Well, antipsychotics, even at low doses,

405
00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:40.600
work by blocking dopamine receptors,

406
00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:43.640
specifically in the mesolimbic pathway of the brain.

407
00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:44.480
Okay.

408
00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:46.960
Dopamine is heavily involved in how the brain

409
00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:49.760
assigns salience or importance to things.

410
00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:51.840
By dialing down the dopamine transmission,

411
00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:55.000
the medication reduces the volume of the background noise.

412
00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:57.760
It stops the brain from flagging that random billboard

413
00:14:57.760 --> 00:14:59.560
as a profound personal message.

414
00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:00.520
That makes total sense.

415
00:15:00.520 --> 00:15:02.920
Yeah, it quiets the perceptual oddities

416
00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:05.560
just enough that the patient can actually engage

417
00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:06.920
with the psychotherapy.

418
00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:08.040
So what does this all mean?

419
00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:09.040
If you're listening to this

420
00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:10.440
and you're trying to mentally organize

421
00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:12.400
this deep dive into cluster A,

422
00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:14.480
let's establish the definitive golden rule.

423
00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:15.320
Okay.

424
00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:16.640
What is the overarching law

425
00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:19.120
that governs all three of these profiles?

426
00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:21.960
Paranoid, schizoid, and schizotypal

427
00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:24.480
and separates them from the heavier psychotic disorders.

428
00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:27.280
The overarching rule is the maintenance of reality testing.

429
00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:29.800
That is the hard boundary in the diagnostic sand.

430
00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:30.640
Reality testing.

431
00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:31.840
Yes.

432
00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:33.800
These are personality disorders,

433
00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:36.160
or in the case of schizotypal,

434
00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:38.320
an attenuated spectrum disorder.

435
00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:40.440
They represent deeply ingrained,

436
00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:44.560
lifelong patterns of perceiving, relating, and coping.

437
00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:45.400
Right.

438
00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:47.640
The psychological lens through which they view the world

439
00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:49.320
might be cracked by fear,

440
00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:51.720
it might be heavily tinted by apathy,

441
00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:53.800
or it might fundamentally distort the light

442
00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:55.480
through magical thinking.

443
00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:57.160
But the eye behind the lens

444
00:15:57.160 --> 00:15:59.640
is still anchored in shared reality.

445
00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:00.680
That's a great metaphor.

446
00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:02.800
None of these three profiles involve

447
00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:05.880
a total fixed break from reality

448
00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:09.560
or the presence of frank, unmistatable psychosis.

449
00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:12.080
They interact with the world from a profound distance,

450
00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:13.800
but they're still in the same world.

451
00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:15.700
Let's bring it all back to where we started.

452
00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:17.160
We are back at the crowded party

453
00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.560
we've taken the deep dive into the clinical mechanics,

454
00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:22.240
and now we understand the hidden architecture of the room.

455
00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:23.680
You have three people standing alone.

456
00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:24.520
Yeah.

457
00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:25.800
The paranoid person is standing alone

458
00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:27.940
because their hypervigilant nervous system

459
00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:30.560
is actively scanning the crowd for threats,

460
00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:33.760
terrified that engagement equals exploitation.

461
00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:36.320
The schizoid person is standing alone.

462
00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:39.520
Because their neurobiology simply lacks

463
00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:42.160
the reward circuitry for social interaction,

464
00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:44.320
they have absolutely zero appetite for the noise.

465
00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:45.160
Exactly.

466
00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:47.220
And the schizotypal person is standing alone

467
00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:50.060
because their brain is assigning magical salience

468
00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:52.420
to random environmental stimuli,

469
00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:54.160
making the regular party chatter

470
00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:56.960
feel overwhelmingly alien to them.

471
00:16:56.960 --> 00:16:59.760
Three identical physical behaviors,

472
00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:02.800
three entirely different psychological universes.

473
00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:03.960
It really makes you wonder

474
00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:06.360
as we look at the trajectory of modern culture.

475
00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:07.200
What do you mean?

476
00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:09.840
Well, as our society moves increasingly online

477
00:17:09.840 --> 00:17:13.000
where asynchronous distant communication is the norm,

478
00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:15.200
where you can work, shop, and exist

479
00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:16.920
without ever sharing physical space

480
00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:19.000
or making eye contact with another human,

481
00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:20.880
are we unintentionally building a world

482
00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:23.140
perfectly designed to accommodate cluster A?

483
00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:23.980
Oh, wow.

484
00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:26.560
And if the environment shifts to perfectly fit the isolation,

485
00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:28.680
does the clinical disorder eventually disappear

486
00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:30.360
into the new baseline of normal?

487
00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:33.040
That is a phenomenal question to leave you with.

488
00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:35.680
If a society no longer demands the party,

489
00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:37.640
do we stop pathologizing the people

490
00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:40.080
who never wanted to attend in the first place?

491
00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:42.320
Next time you see someone standing on the fringes

492
00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:44.080
quietly watching the room,

493
00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:45.720
maybe don't assume you know the mechanics

494
00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:47.240
of why they're standing there.

495
00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:48.960
Thanks for joining us on this deep dive.

496
00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:50.160
We'll see you next time.
